// CALCULATE - modifying filter context
// Generated by scripts/generate-pbip.py - edit there, not here.
//
// Measures 1-5 are already in PL300-Demos.pbip and are what page 6
// renders; they are repeated here so the ladder reads as one piece and
// so it can be lifted into another model by changing the table name.
//
// The variants at the bottom are NOT in the model - adding them is the
// exercise. Modeling > New measure on the CategoryMonthlySales table,
// paste, then check the result against the answer in the comment.

// 1. Sales
// Start here. No CALCULATE at all - the measure just answers whatever
// filter the visual hands it. $6,637,231 with nothing selected.
Sales = SUM ( CategoryMonthlySales[SalesAmount] )

// 2. Sales Camping
// The simplest CALCULATE there is: one filter argument. On a visual with
// Category on rows, all four rows read $3,302,900 - a filter on a column
// that is ALREADY filtered replaces that filter rather than narrowing it.
Sales Camping = CALCULATE ( [Sales], CategoryMonthlySales[Category] = "Camping" )

// 3. Sales Camping Kept
// The same filter wrapped in KEEPFILTERS intersects instead of replacing,
// so only the Camping row survives and the other three go blank. This is
// the contrast that makes the previous measure make sense.
Sales Camping Kept = CALCULATE ( [Sales], KEEPFILTERS ( CategoryMonthlySales[Category] = "Camping" ) )

// 4. Sales All Categories
// Filter arguments can also take filters AWAY. Every row shows the
// $6,637,231 grand total, because the row's own Category filter is gone.
Sales All Categories = CALCULATE ( [Sales], REMOVEFILTERS ( CategoryMonthlySales[Category] ) )

// 5. Category Share %
// Why the previous measure is worth learning: a numerator that changes per
// row over a denominator that does not. Camping 49.8%, Apparel 23.8%,
// Hiking 18.4%, Cooking 8.0% - and they add to 100.0%.
Category Share % = DIVIDE ( [Sales], [Sales All Categories] )

// --------------------------------------------------------------------
// Variants - type these in yourself
// --------------------------------------------------------------------

// 6. Sales Q4
// A filter argument is a condition, not just an equals. $1,566,730 overall;
// on Category rows, Camping $772,957 / Apparel $386,154 / Hiking $285,407 /
// Cooking $122,212.
Sales Q4 = CALCULATE ( [Sales], CategoryMonthlySales[MonthNumber] >= 10 )

// 7. Sales Camping H2
// Two filter arguments are ANDed together: $1,737,262. Compare with
// [Sales] filtered to months 7-12 alone, $3,519,561.
Sales Camping H2 =
CALCULATE ( [Sales], CategoryMonthlySales[Category] = "Camping",
                     CategoryMonthlySales[MonthNumber] >= 7 )

// 8. Sales Everything
// REMOVEFILTERS over the whole TABLE, not one column. Always $6,637,231 -
// including on a visual with Month on rows, where [Sales All Categories]
// still varies because it only clears Category.
Sales Everything = CALCULATE ( [Sales], REMOVEFILTERS ( CategoryMonthlySales ) )

// 9. Sales Visible Categories
// ALLSELECTED clears the filter the visual applied but respects the one the
// user applied. Identical to [Sales All Categories] until you add a Category
// slicer - then pick two categories and watch the denominator follow.
Sales Visible Categories = CALCULATE ( [Sales], ALLSELECTED ( CategoryMonthlySales[Category] ) )
